\begin_layout Title
Ego-Motion from Optic Flow on the Sphere
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A point observer samples optic flow at 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 viewing directions on a unit sphere.
 Objects in the world are static; all retinal motion comes from self-motion.
 Given the flow field, recover the observer's angular velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 and translational velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}$
\end_inset

.
 Depths 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\mathbf{T}\|$
\end_inset

 are coupled (doubling both leaves flow unchanged), so absolute scale is
 unrecoverable from monocular flow alone; we recover 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}$
\end_inset

 up to an unknown positive scalar that is constant as long as the scene
 is.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Conventions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Right-handed world frame: 
\begin_inset Formula $+x$
\end_inset

 forward, 
\begin_inset Formula $+y$
\end_inset

 left, 
\begin_inset Formula $+z$
\end_inset

 up.
 Positive rotations by right-hand rule: 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{x}>0$
\end_inset

 = right roll, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{y}>0$
\end_inset

 = pitch down, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{z}>0$
\end_inset

 = yaw left.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Viewing directions 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{d}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\mathbf{d}\|=1$
\end_inset

, parameterized as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{d}=(\sin\theta\cos\phi,\;\sin\theta\sin\phi,\;\cos\theta),
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 polar from 
\begin_inset Formula $+z$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 azimuth from 
\begin_inset Formula $+x$
\end_inset

 toward 
\begin_inset Formula $+y$
\end_inset

.
 Tangent basis:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{e}_{\theta}=\partial_{\theta}\mathbf{d},\qquad\mathbf{e}_{\phi}=\frac{1}{\sin\theta}\partial_{\phi}\mathbf{d}.
\]

\end_inset

Both are unit length and orthogonal; 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{e}_{\theta}$
\end_inset

 points down and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{e}_{\phi}$
\end_inset

 left on the retina.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Retinal flow is reported as two scalars per point: 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{\text{right}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{\text{up}}$
\end_inset

 (tangent velocities in arc length per time, not coordinate rates — this
 is what T4/T5 cells measure).
 Assembled into a 3D tangent vector:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{u}_{i}=-u_{\text{right},i}\mathbf{e}_{\phi,i}-u_{\text{up},i}\mathbf{e}_{\theta,i}.
\]

\end_inset

The negative signs convert from retinal (right, up) to world-frame 
\begin_inset Formula $(-\mathbf{e}_{\phi},-\mathbf{e}_{\theta})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Forward model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{u}_{i}=-\boldsymbol{\Omega}\times\mathbf{d}_{i}-\frac{1}{Z_{i}}\Pi_{i}\mathbf{T},\qquad\Pi_{i}=I-\mathbf{d}_{i}\mathbf{d}_{i}^{\intercal}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Rotation term.
 
\series default
Observer rotating at 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 sees the world counter-rotate at 
\begin_inset Formula $-\boldsymbol{\Omega}$
\end_inset

, so viewing directions drift at 
\begin_inset Formula $-\boldsymbol{\Omega}\times\mathbf{d}_{i}$
\end_inset

 (rigid-body velocity formula applied in the observer frame).
 Independent of depth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Translation term.
 
\series default
A world point at 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{i}\mathbf{d}_{i}$
\end_inset

 moves at 
\begin_inset Formula $-\mathbf{T}$
\end_inset

 relative to the observer.
 The projector 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{i}$
\end_inset

 extracts the tangential component (radial motion is invisible — it changes
 distance but not image location).
 Dividing by 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{i}$
\end_inset

 converts 3D velocity to angular velocity on the unit sphere: near points
 produce large flow, far points small flow.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{a}\times\mathbf{b}=[\mathbf{a}]_{\times}\mathbf{b}$
\end_inset

 and antisymmetry, the rotation term rewrites as 
\begin_inset Formula $[\mathbf{d}_{i}]_{\times}\boldsymbol{\Omega}$
\end_inset

, which is linear in 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 and enables least-squares:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{u}_{i}=[\mathbf{d}_{i}]_{\times}\boldsymbol{\Omega}-\frac{1}{Z_{i}}\Pi_{i}\mathbf{T}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Algorithm
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
1.
 Rotation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ignore translation.
 With dense sphere coverage and depth statistically independent of direction,
 the translational term averages to zero in the normal equations.
 Stack 
\begin_inset Formula $A=[[\mathbf{d}_{1}]_{\times};\dots;[\mathbf{d}_{N}]_{\times}]\in\mathbb{R}^{3N\times3}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{3N}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{*}=(A^{\intercal}A)^{-1}A^{\intercal}\mathbf{u}.
\]

\end_inset

Depth-anisotropic scenes (walls, ground planes) bias this; an optional nonlinear
 refinement can clean it up.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
2.
 De-rotate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{v}_{i}:=\mathbf{u}_{i}-[\mathbf{d}_{i}]_{\times}\boldsymbol{\Omega}^{*}\approx-\frac{1}{Z_{i}}\Pi_{i}\mathbf{T}.
\]

\end_inset

Both sides are tangent to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{d}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.
 Translation direction via SVD
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At each point, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is colinear with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{i}\mathbf{T}$
\end_inset

, so their cross product vanishes:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
[\mathbf{v}_{i}]_{\times}\Pi_{i}\mathbf{T}=\mathbf{0}.
\]

\end_inset

The cross product kills the unknown 
\begin_inset Formula $1/Z_{i}$
\end_inset

, decoupling 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}$
\end_inset

 from the depths.
 Stack:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C=\begin{bmatrix}[\mathbf{v}_{1}]_{\times}\Pi_{1}\\
\vdots\\{}
[\mathbf{v}_{N}]_{\times}\Pi_{N}
\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{3N\times3},\qquad C\mathbf{T}=\mathbf{0}.
\]

\end_inset

Homogeneous, so solve 
\begin_inset Formula $\min_{\|\mathbf{T}\|=1}\|C\mathbf{T}\|^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The minimizer is the right singular vector of 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 with the smallest singular value; call it 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mathbf{t}}$
\end_inset

.
 Sign is unresolved (cross product is insensitive to antiparallel vs parallel)
 — fix by enforcing depth positivity: flip 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mathbf{t}}$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i}(\mathbf{v}_{i}\cdot\Pi_{i}\hat{\mathbf{t}})\|\Pi_{i}\hat{\mathbf{t}}\|^{2}>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
4.
 Scaled inverse depths
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With direction fixed, the recoverable per-point magnitude is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\rho_{i}:=\frac{\|\mathbf{T}\|}{Z_{i}},
\]

\end_inset

so the model becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{v}_{i}=-\rho_{i}\Pi_{i}\hat{\mathbf{t}}$
\end_inset

.
 Least-squares for each scalar:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\rho_{i}=-\frac{\mathbf{v}_{i}\cdot\Pi_{i}\hat{\mathbf{t}}}{\|\Pi_{i}\hat{\mathbf{t}}\|^{2}}.
\]

\end_inset

Positive 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{i}$
\end_inset

 means the point is in front of the observer (guaranteed in aggregate by
 the sign-disambiguation step).
 Absolute 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\mathbf{T}\|$
\end_inset

 and absolute 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{i}$
\end_inset

 cannot be separated without external information (stereo, IMU, known depth).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
5.
 Final translation estimate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Aggregate the 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{i}$
\end_inset

 into a single scalar and multiply by the direction:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{T}^{*}=\rho_{\text{agg}}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{t}},\qquad\rho_{\text{agg}}=\underset{i\in\mathcal{I}}{\text{median}}\,\rho_{i},\qquad\mathcal{I}=\{i:\|\Pi_{i}\hat{\mathbf{t}}\|^{2}>\tau\}.
\]

\end_inset

The filter set 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}$
\end_inset

 drops points near the focus of expansion, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{i}\hat{\mathbf{t}}\approx\mathbf{0}$
\end_inset

 makes 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{i}$
\end_inset

 ill-conditioned (threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau\approx0.1$
\end_inset

 works well).
 Median over mean because the depth distribution has a long inverse tail:
 a single nearby point dominates the mean but moves the median negligibly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a fixed environment, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{\text{agg}}=\|\mathbf{T}\|\cdot\text{median}(1/Z_{i})$
\end_inset

, and the median-depth factor is a scene-level constant approximately invariant
 to small rotations (which permute the 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{i}$
\end_inset

 values across sensors but do not change the set).
 Therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}^{*}(t)\propto\mathbf{T}(t)$
\end_inset

 across time while the observer stays in the same environment.
 Absolute scale is not recovered, but relative speeds and directions are.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Two offset eyes (binocular)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The derivation above places the sensor at the observer's center of rotation.
 A real head carries two eyes at fixed body-frame offsets 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{r}_{L},\mathbf{r}_{R}$
\end_inset

 from the center of mass.
 For a rigid body with angular velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 and center-of-mass velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}$
\end_inset

, the eye at offset 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{r}_{e}$
\end_inset

 moves at 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{v}_{e}=\mathbf{T}+\boldsymbol{\Omega}\times\mathbf{r}_{e}$
\end_inset

.
 So eye 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 sees flow
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{u}_{i}=-\boldsymbol{\Omega}\times\mathbf{d}_{i}-\frac{1}{Z_{i}}\Pi_{i}\big(\mathbf{T}+\boldsymbol{\Omega}\times\mathbf{r}_{e}\big),\qquad i\in\text{eye }e.
\]

\end_inset

Each eye shares 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 and the same spherical basis; only the translation term gains the rigid-body
 velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}\times\mathbf{r}_{e}$
\end_inset

.
 Write the per-eye effective translation as 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}_{e}=\mathbf{T}+\mathbf{b}_{e}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{b}_{e}=\boldsymbol{\Omega}\times\mathbf{r}_{e}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
What changes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Steps 1–2 are unchanged: rotation is shared across the eyes and is independent
 of the offset, so stacking both eyes' columns in the least-squares for 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 and the per-point de-rotation work exactly as before.
 Only the translation step changes, and it stays a 
\series bold
single joint optimization
\series default
 over both eyes — the eyes are 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 reduced to per-eye directions and then merged.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Joint translation with absolute scale
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The de-rotated flow 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{v}_{i}\approx-\frac{1}{Z_{i}}\Pi_{i}\mathbf{T}_{e}$
\end_inset

 is colinear with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{i}\mathbf{T}_{e}$
\end_inset

, so the cross product vanishes and the unknown depth drops out:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
[\mathbf{v}_{i}]_{\times}\Pi_{i}\big(\mathbf{T}+\mathbf{b}_{e}\big)=\mathbf{0}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\underbrace{[\mathbf{v}_{i}]_{\times}\Pi_{i}}_{C_{i}}\,\mathbf{T}=-C_{i}\,\mathbf{b}_{e}.
\]

\end_inset

The crucial point is that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{b}_{e}=\boldsymbol{\Omega}\times\mathbf{r}_{e}$
\end_inset

 is known in 
\series bold
absolute
\series default
 units: 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 comes out of step 1 with no scale ambiguity and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{r}_{e}$
\end_inset

 is fixed geometry.
 The right-hand side is therefore a known, non-zero vector, turning the
 homogeneous (direction-only) monocular problem into an inhomogeneous one
 that pins down 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}$
\end_inset

 outright.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Stack the constraint over 
\series bold
every column of every eye
\series default
 and solve one least-squares for the body translation 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}$
\end_inset

 directly:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C=\begin{bmatrix}\vdots\\
C_{i}\\
\vdots
\end{bmatrix},\qquad\mathbf{d}=\begin{bmatrix}\vdots\\
-C_{i}\mathbf{b}_{e(i)}\\
\vdots
\end{bmatrix},\qquad\mathbf{T}^{*}=\arg\min_{\mathbf{T}}\,\|C\mathbf{T}-\mathbf{d}\|^{2}=(C^{\intercal}C)^{-1}C^{\intercal}\mathbf{d}.
\]

\end_inset

This uses all columns at once — each weighted by its own flow magnitude
 — rather than collapsing each eye to a single estimated direction first.
 The normal matrix accumulates per eye as 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\intercal}C=\sum_{e}\sum_{i\in e}C_{i}^{\intercal}C_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\intercal}\mathbf{d}=-\sum_{e}\big(\sum_{i\in e}C_{i}^{\intercal}C_{i}\big)\mathbf{b}_{e}$
\end_inset

.
 Because the sign of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{b}_{e}$
\end_inset

 is fixed, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}^{*}$
\end_inset

 comes out with the correct sign as well — no separate depth-positivity
 disambiguation is needed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Focus-of-expansion mask.
\series default
 Columns near each eye's focus of expansion have 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{i}\hat{\mathbf{t}}_{e}\approx\mathbf{0}$
\end_inset

 and carry almost no translation signal.
 A second pass drops them — keep only 
\begin_inset Formula $\|\Pi_{i}\hat{\mathbf{t}}_{e}\|^{2}=1-(\mathbf{d}_{i}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{t}}_{e})^{2}>\tau$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mathbf{t}}_{e}$
\end_inset

 the direction of the first-pass 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}^{*}+\mathbf{b}_{e}$
\end_inset

 — and re-solves (
\begin_inset Formula $\tau\approx0.1$
\end_inset

).
 The per-point inverse depth 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{i}=\|\mathbf{T}_{e}\|/Z_{i}=-\mathbf{v}_{i}\cdot\Pi_{i}\hat{\mathbf{t}}_{e}/\|\Pi_{i}\hat{\mathbf{t}}_{e}\|^{2}$
\end_inset

 then follows from the final 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Degeneracy.
\series default
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}\times(\mathbf{r}_{L}-\mathbf{r}_{R})\approx\mathbf{0}$
\end_inset

 — no rotation, or the inter-eye baseline parallel to 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 — both eyes see the same translation direction, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{d}\to\mathbf{0}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 becomes rank-deficient along that shared direction (reciprocal condition
 number of 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\intercal}C$
\end_inset

 near zero).
 Absolute scale is then unrecoverable and the solver falls back to the homogeneou
s single-sphere estimate (direction via the smallest right singular vector,
 magnitude via median 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

).
 A single eye, or zero offsets, reduces exactly to the monocular result.
\end_layout

